  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 332 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nMilder conditions are expected with near to above normal \ntemperatures through Wednesday. Seasonably cool temperatures return \nlate in the week with dry weather favored through much of the period.\n\n(Monday through Wednesday) \n\nThe northern stream remains active and departed to the north with \nseveral upper level disturbances traversing the northern CONUS and \nsouthern Canadian Provinces. Meanwhile, a broad, closed low evolves \ninto and open wave as it slowly crosses Texas moves eastward into \nthe Gulf States. The axis to a low amplitude mid-level ridge \napproaches the Mississippi Valley Monday, being closely followed by \na mid-level trough. Both features progressively become more muted \nthrough Wednesday as mid-level temperatures in the mid-teens (C) \nover the northern Plains are moderated before reaching the \nMississippi Valley thanks to northwesterly flow aloft. The only hint \nat rain comes late Wednesday as a weak surface low develops near the \nMissouri/Arkansas border and quickly moves eastward. The system is \nnot well organized at this stage, lacking both moisture and broad \nsupport for beneficial rainfall. Highs are expected to be in the 60s \neach afternoon. Chilly morning lows in the 30s/40s Monday give way \nto the 40s Tuesday and Wednesday morning. \n\n(Thursday through Saturday) \n\nBoth GFS/ECM global guidance show a long wave trough enveloping the \neastern CONUS through the later portion of the period, though its \ndevelopment and exact evolution give rise to increasing spread \nthrough the later portion of the period. Despite the disagreement, \nthe general characteristics are becoming more clear, including \nseasonably cool or chilly temperature late in the week into next \nweekend. LREF ensembles show the greatest spread in the upper level \npressure pattern extending from the Dakotas through Oklahoma, \nindicating uncertainty in the depth or breadth of its influence. \nECMWF/GFS Ensembles do agree, in characteristic, that cooler \nconditions arrive by Thursday with the coolest air arriving Friday. \nSpread decrease briefly when surrounding the cooler air before \nexpanding once again Saturday, suggesting timing differences in the \neastward progression of the longwave trough. Several embedded \nshortwaves rotate down the western side of the trough, though the \nnumber, placement, and timing of these features are not well handled \nat this distance in time, though low end precipitation potential \nmay become more evident as we progress through the week.",
